Why can't the EC2 boot directly from the AMI?

The AMI is a template, not the normal writable disk of the running EC2.

For example, you select:

Ubuntu AMI

AWS uses that AMI to create:

Root EBS
└── Ubuntu OS

Then the EC2 uses that EBS volume as its boot disk.









Case 1: Snapshot only the root volume
EC2
 └── Root EBS
      ├── OS
      └── Files

Snapshot → backs up OS + files on that root volume.

Case 2: Snapshot all EBS volumes
EC2
 ├── Root EBS
 │    └── OS
 │
 └── Data EBS
      └── Application/Data

Snapshots → back up OS + application/data.

So the difference is simply:

One EBS snapshot = backup of that particular volume.

Snapshots of all EBS volumes = backup of all those volumes.

They are not two different types of snapshots.




If you mean how to restore your EC2 after deleting it using a snapshot, do it like this:

1. Before deleting the old EC2

Go to:

EC2 → Volumes

Find the volume attached to your EC2.

EC2
 ↓
EBS Volume
 ↓
Actions → Create snapshot

Give the snapshot a name and create it.

2. Delete the old EC2

You can terminate the old EC2.

Your snapshot remains if you created it separately.

3. Create a new EC2

Go to:

EC2 → Launch instance

Create a new EC2 normally.

It will automatically get a new root EBS volume.

New EC2
   |
   └── New Root EBS
4. Restore your old volume

Go to:

EC2 → Snapshots

Select your snapshot:

Actions → Create volume from snapshot

Choose the same Availability Zone as your new EC2.

Snapshot
   ↓
Create Volume
   ↓
Old EBS data restored
5. Attach it to the new EC2

Go to:

EC2 → Volumes → select the new volume → Actions → Attach volume

Select your new EC2.

Now:

New EC2
 ├── New Root EBS
 │     └── New OS
 │
 └── Restored EBS
       └── Old data

Then log into the EC2 and mount the restored volume.

Very important

If your snapshot was of the old root volume and you want the old OS itself, the process is different:
you can create a volume from that snapshot and use it as the root volume when launching the new EC2. You don't necessarily 
need to attach it as a secondary data disk.




If you mean how to take snapshots separately for the root volume and the data volume, do this:

Example EC2
EC2
├── /dev/xvda → Root EBS
│                OS + files
│
└── /dev/xvdf → Data EBS
                 Application/data
1. Snapshot only the Root EBS

Go to:

EC2 → Volumes

Find the Root EBS volume (/dev/xvda).

Then:

Actions → Create snapshot

Now you have:

Root EBS
   ↓
Root Snapshot
   ↓
OS + files backed up
2. Snapshot only the Data EBS

Again go to:

EC2 → Volumes

Find the Data EBS volume (/dev/xvdf).

Then:

Actions → Create snapshot

Now you have:

Data EBS
   ↓
Data Snapshot
   ↓
Application/data backed up

So you will have two separate snapshots:

Snapshot 1 → Root EBS → OS + files
Snapshot 2 → Data EBS → Application/data

Later, you can restore either one independently. 

If you mean the old volume contains the OS

You cannot simply remove the new root volume and run the EC2 from nothing. The EC2 needs a root/boot volume.

Instead, use the old snapshot to create the root volume for the new EC2.

Old EC2
   ↓
Old Root EBS
   ↓
Snapshot
   ↓
Create AMI / use snapshot for root
   ↓
Launch New EC2
   ↓
Old OS + Old files

Then your new EC2 can look like:

New EC2
   |
   └── Old Root Volume
         ├── Old OS
         └── Old files/data
If the old volume is only a data volume

Then you can keep the new EC2's root volume and attach only the old data volume:

New EC2
├── New Root EBS → OS
│
└── Old EBS → Old data

So the answer depends on whether your old volume is the root/OS volume or just a data volume.


Situation 1 — Old EBS contains only data

You need the new root EBS to run the EC2:

New EC2
│
├── New Root EBS → OS        ← required to boot
│
└── Old EBS → Old data       ← what you want

You cannot detach the new root EBS while using that EC2.

Situation 2 — Old EBS contains the old OS

Then you can make the old EBS the root volume of the new EC2.

Old Snapshot
     ↓
Old EBS
     ↓
New EC2
     ↓
Old EBS = Root EBS

Then there is no separate new root EBS that you need to keep:

New EC2
   │
   └── Old EBS → Old OS + old data

So if you're trying to restore the old EC2 exactly as it was, you should restore the old root-volume 
snapshot as the root volume, rather than creating a new EC2 first and then attaching it as a secondary volume.



while creating the new EC2, you can select the old snapshot/AMI as the root volume source.

The easiest way is to create an AMI from the old EC2/root-volume snapshot, then launch the new EC2 from that AMI.

Old EC2
   ↓
Old Root EBS
   ↓
Create Snapshot
   ↓
Create AMI
   ↓
Launch New EC2 using that AMI
   ↓
New EC2
   |
   └── Root EBS
       ├── Old OS
       └── Old data

So the new EC2 can have only one EBS volume, and that volume contains your old OS + old data.

In the Launch EC2 screen

When you select your custom AMI, AWS automatically uses the AMI's root volume as the root EBS volume.

You don't need to:

Create new EC2
   ↓
Attach old EBS
   ↓
Detach new root EBS

Instead, do:

Old Snapshot → AMI → New EC2

This is the cleaner way to restore the old EC2.






